<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1915 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Solidarity Forever</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song from 1915 that asks working people to stand together in a union; it is still sung at union meetings and marches.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ralph Chaplin</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional; sung to the tune of &quot;John Brown's Body&quot; and &quot;The Battle Hymn of the Republic&quot;</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Industrial Workers of the World; later the labour movement of the United States, Canada, Australia and New Zealand</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Chicago, Illinois, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>begun 1913; completed 15 January 1915</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Ralph Chaplin, completed 15 January 1915 and published by the IWW)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Ralph Chaplin began writing &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-forever">Solidarity Forever</a>&quot; in 1913, while he was reporting on the Paint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>–Cabin Creek strike of 1912 in Kanawha County, West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. He was moved by the resolve and high spirits of the striking miners and their families, who had lived in tents for a year through a violent strike in which about 50 people were killed on both sides. He finished the song in Chicago on 15 January 1915, the day of a hunger demonstration. Chaplin was a committed member of the Industrial Workers of the World, the &quot;Wobblies&quot;, and wrote for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a>, the union's paper in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and drew its cartoons. He set his words to the tune of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a>&quot; and &quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a>&quot;.</p><p>The song puts into verse the IWW's &quot;Preamble&quot;, printed inside the front cover of every Little Red Songbook, which opens: &quot;The working class and the employing class have nothing in common.&quot; The Preamble called for workers to organise as a class, take possession of the means of production and abolish the wage system; it held that trade unions, by negotiating contracts, misled workers into thinking they shared interests with employers, and it rejected participation in electoral politics, and spoke of &quot;forming the structure of the new society within the shell of the old&quot;. Chaplin's six stanzas follow that analysis: two classes with nothing in common, the workers as the makers of the world's wealth and its rightful owners, union solidarity as their strength, and a new world brought to birth &quot;from the ashes of the old&quot;.</p><p>The song spread far beyond the IWW. Other unions, among them the AFL–CIO, adopted it, and it is still sung at union meetings, protests and rallies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and at conferences of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australian-labor-party">Australian Labor Party</a> and Canada's New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a>. It has been translated into French, German, Polish, Spanish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>. Chaplin was not pleased. Late in life, by then an opponent of the Communists in the labour movement, he wrote &quot;Why I Wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-forever">Solidarity Forever</a>&quot;, in which he complained that a song &quot;uniquely our own&quot; had become &quot;a singing commercial&quot; for the industrial unionism that followed the Wagner Act, and that &quot;when the labor movement ceases to be a Cause and becomes a business, the end product can hardly be called progress.&quot; The IWW's songbook even carried a satire, &quot;Aristocracy Forever&quot;, mocking the AFL–CIO's use of the song.</p><p>Singers have shaped it to their own times. Many leave out the second and fourth stanzas, the most radical. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>'s version drops them and rewrites the last; since the 1970s women union members have added verses about women's work and wages, a Canadian set adds verses against racism, and the centennial edition of the Little Red Songbook includes two new verses by Steve Suffet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> Phillips, Pete Seeger and John Darnielle are among those who have performed it, and it is sung in the 2014 film Pride, about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> group Lesbians and Gays Support the Miners in the 1984–1985 UK miners' strike.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A rallying song of industrial unionism: the workers who built the world's wealth are told that alone they are weak, that against the owners they have nothing in common, and that only the union, held together, makes them strong. The mood is defiant and confident, ending in the hope of a new world built from the ruins of the old.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When the Union's inspiration</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Through the Workers' blood shall run</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There can be no power greater</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Anywhere beneath the sun.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Yet what force on earth is weaker</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Than the feeble strength of one?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But the Union makes us strong.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Solidarity forever!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Solidarity forever!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Solidarity forever!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For the Union makes us strong.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Is there aught we hold in common</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With the greedy parasite,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who would lash us into serfdom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And would crush us with his might?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Is there anything left for us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But to organize and fight?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For the Union makes us strong.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 46 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It is we who plowed the prairies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Built the cities where they trade,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dug the mines, and built the workshops,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Endless miles of railroad laid.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now we stand outcast and starving</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Mid the wonders we have made;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But the Union makes us strong!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All the world that's owned by idle drones,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Is ours and ours alone.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We have laid the wide foundations.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Built it skywards stone by srone.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It is ours and not to slave in,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But to master and to own,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>While the Union makes us strong.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They have taken untold millions</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That they never toiled to earn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But without our brain and muscle</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Not a single wheel can turn!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We can break their galling shackles—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Gain our freedom when we learn</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That the Union makes us strong.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In our hands is placed a power</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Greater than their greedy gold—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Greater than the might of armies,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Magnified a thousandfold;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We can bring to birth the new world</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From the ashes of the old,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For the Union makes us strong!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Ralph Chaplin, When the Leaves Come Out (1917). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/When_the_Leaves_Come_Out%2FSolidarity_Forever" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
